PATCH 627: resolve original DMA initialization port tables through DS (board:0003).

Original 2810 reads WORD DS:(table+ECX*2) for mask 12ff, flip-flop
131f, mode 130f, count 12ef and address 12df. The decompile instead
reads bare host offsets and invents the count table from &FUN_0000_12eb.
Use the shared fist_ext_addr owner for those exact original data operands.
The existing page access is already based at module+12cc+3 = 12cf;
its width, byte OUTs, count/word-address branch and AX return stay intact.

The complete original 534:536-ms instruction trace reaches 2810 from 138d
at cycle 16066813 and its RET 28a4 at 16067050. It records all nine
byte OUTs: mask channel 1, clear flip-flop, mode 59, count 07ff,
address 2de0, page 00 and unmask. The immutable six WORD port tables
also match the paging-aware original snapshot and provisioned image.
This is a 2048-byte DMA ring, separate from the 1024-byte DSP block.

The unchanged-parent regression reaches native SIGSEGV and eight wrong
WASM port addresses. The actual patched routine now programs the real
shared SB/DMA owner; an independent endpoint compiles DOSBox's original
DMA/DSP owners and receives the captured original OUTs. Compare every
OUT, AX return, entire untouched module state, both complete 1024-byte
PCM8 device inputs, DMA registers/terminal-count reload, DSP state and
IRQ acknowledgements on both targets. No game samples are added.

Scope is this reached initialization dependency, not final mixed PCM.
Actual startup/service dispatch, protected IRQ/IF, other device variants,
instruction/event timing and final mixer integration remain open.
No measured cycle interval is inserted as a delay.
